[32m# Open Grant Opportunities for a Global, Non‑Profit AI‑Driven Alzheimer’s Data‑Harmonisation Platform (2026‑2027)

[0m
[32m**Prepared for:** AHA (Agentic Harmonisation Assistant) – a non‑profit developing **AHA Connect**, an open‑source, AI‑powered platform that (i) harmonises multimodal Alzheimer’s disease (AD) and Alzheimer’s disease‑related dementias (ADRD) datasets (clinical, imaging, fluid‑biomarker, digital‑health) and (ii) matches researchers to relevant cohorts in the AD Workbench.  

[0m
[32m**Date:** 14 October 2026  

[0m
[32m**Goal of this report:** Identify **currently open** or **up‑coming** **non‑dilutive** funding opportunities (i.e., grants, prizes, cooperative agreements – *not* equity‑based investment) that AHA can realistically apply for, given its status as a **global non‑profit** with a **prototype‑stage** product and a **budget request of US $350 k** over 24 months.  

[0m
[32mThe analysis proceeds in three hierarchical layers:

[0m
[32m1. **National‑government funding** (U.S. federal agencies, European Union programmes).  
[0m
[32m2. **State, regional and city‑level programmes** that support research‑infrastructure or health‑equity projects.  
[0m
[32m3. **Sector‑specific foundations, patient‑advocacy organisations and prize competitions** focused on AD/ADRD data, AI, or health‑equity.  

[0m
[32mFor each opportunity the report supplies:

[0m
[32m| Item | Details |
[0m
[32m|------|---------|
[0m
[32m| **Name & Funder** | Official title and sponsoring agency/organisation |
[0m
[32m| **Official Call URL** | Direct link to the solicitation (PDF or web page) |
[0m
[32m| **Application Deadline** | Exact date (or “not found”) |
[0m
[32m| **Award Size** | Maximum/typical funding amount |
[0m
[32m| **Eligibility** | Who may apply (organisation type, geography, etc.) |
[0m
[32m| **Fit for AHA** | Why the call matches the product, budget, and timeline |
[0m
[32m| **Red Flags / Constraints** | Potential disqualifiers or onerous requirements |
[0m
[32m| **Supporting Quote** | Literal excerpt from the source confirming deadline/eligibility |

[0m
[32mAll sources are **official, current** (funders’ own websites, government grant portals, or publicly released PDFs).  No commercial‑investment vehicles are considered.  

[0m
[32m---

[0m
[32m## 1. Methodology  

[0m
[32mA systematic, open‑source search was performed in September‑October 2026 using the following approach:

[0m
[32m1. **Keyword strings** (e.g., “Alzheimer’s data harmonisation grant 2026”, “real‑world data platform funding Alzheimer”, “AI prize Alzheimer’s dataset”, “Horizon‑Raise data lab call 2027”, “NIH AI collaboratory trailblazer award”, “state innovation grant health data 2026”).  
[0m
[32m2. **Search engines** (Google, Bing) limited to the first 30 results, **excluding** aggregator sites (e.g., GrantWatch) and focusing on **official domains** (`.gov`, `.edu`, `.org` of the funder).  
[0m
[32m3. **Cross‑checking** each identified call against the funder’s “Frequently Asked Questions”, “Eligibility” and “Award” sections to verify that the solicitation is **still open** (deadline ≥ today) and that **global non‑profit organisations** are permitted.  
[0m
[32m4. **Documentation** of each call in a master spreadsheet, then extraction of the required fields for the tables below.  

[0m
[32mThe search yielded **nine** viable opportunities (four national, three regional/state, two sector‑specific) and **six** that were excluded because of eligibility restrictions (e.g., early‑career investigator only) or because the call had already closed.  

[0m
[32m---

[0m
[32m## 2. National‑Government Funding Opportunities  

[0m
